The dashboard answers on the cluster network only. Forward it to the Mac:

  kubectl -n kubernetes-dashboard port-forward svc/kubernetes-dashboard 8443:443

then open https://localhost:8443/ and sign in with the token written above,
for example with `pbcopy < <that file>`. The certificate is self-signed, so the
browser warns once. `kubectl proxy` does not work for this: the dashboard
refuses a token sign-in over plain HTTP.

It is not exposed as a LoadBalancer on purpose. A LoadBalancer on ferry is the
Mac's LAN address, and this is a cluster-admin console.

This is the retired Kubernetes Dashboard; `ferry addons enable headlamp` is
its successor, with the same kind of sign-in.
